# Backend Auth

Reference: `api/docs/auth-development.md` and `api/README.md`.

## Boundaries

- Controllers only route HTTP and map results; no EF queries, transactions, or auth business rules.
- Return DTOs only — never EF entities.
- Identity (users/passwords/refresh tokens) is separate from dynamic authorization (permissions/groups/OUs).
- ASP.NET Identity roles are schema scaffolding only — never use roles for access control.

## Tokens and cookies

- Access JWT: short-lived; validate issuer, audience, signature, lifetime.
- Refresh tokens: store hashes only in PostgreSQL; rotate on use; revoke family on reuse/race.
- Browser refresh cookie: HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Path=/api/identity, Secure outside Development.
- Do not put refresh tokens in JSON responses for browser clients.
- Cookie POSTs to refresh/revoke require trusted Origin/Referer when the refresh cookie is present.
- Login/register/mfa/verify always require trusted Origin/Referer (reject when both are missing).

## Authorization

- Protect admin endpoints with specific `authorization.*` or resource permissions.
- Use `[RequirePermissionOnUnit]` for resource APIs that are organization-unit scoped.
- Expose caller-accessible OUs via `GET /api/authorization/me/organization-units`; business OU APIs need `[RequirePermissionOnUnit]` plus Application `IAuthorizationScopeService` checks (catalog does not auto-protect).
- Permission mutations must audit and bump authorization state version after PostgreSQL commit.
- Never remove the last active member of a privileged group (atomic remove under row lock).
- If the shared authz version bump fails after commit, activate cache bypass so replicas do not serve stale Redis entries.

## Redis

- PostgreSQL is the source of truth. Redis is only for authz cache/version, MFA tickets, and distributed auth rate limits.
- Do not introduce a generic `BaseRedisService`.
- Prefer `Redis:ConnectionString`, with fallback to `ConnectionStrings:Redis`.
- Every Redis key needs the app prefix and a TTL when the value is temporary.
- If the shared authz version cannot be read (or cache bypass is active), bypass distributed cache and load from PostgreSQL.
- Login/MFA distributed rate limits should fail closed when Redis is unavailable.

## Security hygiene

- Never log passwords, refresh tokens, access tokens, MFA secrets, or MFA tickets.
- Use `ProblemDetails` with stable `code` values.
- Keep rate limiting on auth endpoints; do not partition by client-supplied account headers.
- Outside Development: require concrete AllowedHosts, HTTPS Client:Origins, non-dev JWT signing key, and Redis.
